#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045486 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/hard #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045492 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/blank #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1007 "Calculus II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Calculus II course builds upon the differential calculus covered in Calculus I , providing in-depth coverage of integral calculus including integration techniques, applications of integration. Advanced topics such as series convergence tests, Taylor series, calculus with parametric and polar equations, and introductory differential equations are also covered. This comprehensive course will prepare students for further studies in advanced mathematics, engineering, statistics, machine learning, and other fields requiring a solid foundation in integral calculus." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1009 "Further your understanding of calculus: master advanced integration techniques, model real-world situations using differential equations, and more." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1012 106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1013 "CA2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "4965f9cc-fd21-5d50-b441-6e80983b17dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region Between Two Curves About the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1033 3641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "aa7fefd1-2912-58e4-b2a0-bbf07ec60fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region About the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1033 415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "32cf9e59-1dd8-51df-b1af-51ac52d78285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotation About the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1033 1084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7d6f9e38-bd31-5a75-ae9b-e0749e0230ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094609 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "45d1f004-828d-5d62-932b-959a70b00f4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096583 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096583 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=2x^3-8x^2+8x$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d70bf5e-9f4f-5e73-8105-c3273eb9e735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096583 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+8x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-{2}^{4}+\\frac{4\\cdot {2}^{3}}{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{2}+\\frac{{2}^{5}}{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{5}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot 32(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096584 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8854" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096584 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=3x-x^2$ and $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b28d5323-bb98-5eb9-b08f-f7ea663498e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096584 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(x)=3x-x^2$ and $g(x)=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2.$ \n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 3x-{x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ 3x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/5a/5a9fd40682646575ada14297ef7e1de766852f99dbdab986d5baa1fb5c2feb94.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=\\dfrac12 x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=3x-x^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be calculated as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([3x-{x}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(\\frac{2x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\pi [(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{4})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096828 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cbc92044-1eab-5287-b92c-b0f6b9359b85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Volume of a Solid When a Region is Enclosed Between Two Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096829 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9a610efd-0cc9-5bec-bb60-d731507c845a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1045 "The Shell Method: Rotation About the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ccfc0ad3-bc0a-5966-a332-0811e12e1581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097106 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $y=-x+\\frac{5}{2}.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4cf3168-db28-57ab-9733-9a47f7b36ec6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097106 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(x)=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $g(x)=-x+\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & =-x+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/27/27919b5cbe7c0d8b7f7c58a9378f5aa3d30e5466c63b1b17d8a1b6af028ecf66.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=-x+\\frac{5}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}x([2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]-[-x+\\frac{5}{2}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}x(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}(-{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-5x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+2{x}^{3}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{625}{4}+250-\\frac{125}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{4}+2-\\frac{5}{2})] \\\\  & =\\pi [\\frac{125}{4}-(-\\frac{3}{4})] \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097763 #edb/ref 1061 613461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47ccde93-5ae1-56aa-9668-0004743ca5c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097764 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097764 #edb/ref 1061 613462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097764 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cbdbf4e-5ffe-533e-b930-ebf96a148350" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097764 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097765 #edb/ref 1061 613465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0597bf4-07bf-5102-809b-eaa8335cac72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097766 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097766 #edb/ref 1061 613466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097766 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5f897f0-7cbe-5647-9a73-c540e707a682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097766 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097767 #edb/ref 1061 613472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7002a08-0710-5fdb-b840-e9945a5f8629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097882 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/e9/e9902ba13717e576f07a15396e6a78fd823a3ad92762688d7802cc7ac95849c0.png)\n\nWe can compute the volume of the solid using the shell method. We divide the interval from $x=a$ to $x=b$ on the $x$-axis to get vertical slices. Consider one such slice of width $\\Delta{x},$ as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $y$-axis, it forms a solid shell (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/c7/c7be68342d347b8ef48e3cf2b8e20b88bedca594fc3d4846f03b5391ae8dbfc2.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $x.$ The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{x}$ and its height is $y=f(x)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its height is $y=f(x)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{x},$ and its length is $2\\pi x$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/a4/a40f23b110ab24c55665784219b7013e587487f88d8d7857260458b9c7a20d2d.png)\n\n \n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{shell}} & ≈{V}_{\\text{rec}} \\\\  & =2\\pi x\\cdot y\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\pi xf(x)Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of all the shells will approximate the total volume we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{x} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097882 #edb/ref 1065 5287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097882 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a79e6b6c-f1bd-5918-96f5-7a2b9a19608e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097882 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097883 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $y=f(x)$ on the top, $y=g(x)$ on the bottom, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. We assume that $f(x) \\geq g(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b].$     \n\n![](images/37/372ece19360ca4f48a5ef48637e49d8f401ee2f1fd562f13c481c767a536bf6c.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([f(x)]-[g(x)])\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097883 #edb/ref 1065 5291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097883 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d08e59d8-b71f-516e-8909-6d8ab812bb5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097883 #edb/ref 1067 "Rotating the Region Between Two Curves About the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097884 #edb/ref 1064 "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $y = f(x)$ about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $y$-axis:\n\n![](images/ad/ad563abd1e7f4a413337dc9527d2c3703d4851e756f385a75797c6f5962cf51f.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}xf(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine the volume using the disc method, we would first have to express our curve as two functions of $y,$ one for the right-hand side and one for the left-hand side, as follows: \n\n![](images/a8/a841ed797699e8f20b494baa6ff888eb42ccd4715e17733e495405df5ca8c5dc.svg)\n\n![](images/ee/ee7ef70a2a280c42ceea9dc6b8bdb3e3bafbfec0256350cbabde287d33d9884b.png)\n\nThe required volume equals the difference between the volumes generated when the right and left curves are rotated about the $y$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097884 #edb/ref 1065 5294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097884 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ba06c616-2c80-5ffb-a64d-eda65b594b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097884 #edb/ref 1067 "Advantages of the Shell Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540531 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540531 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540531 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04c497e8-eb24-5f03-a516-20786f265c87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540536 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{125\\pi }{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07f0467a-b008-57da-a9a3-fe734f1b4c9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540547 #edb/ref 1080 "44\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540547 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540547 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bb55645-fb6e-544b-b276-80013b78e710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540564 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{5})\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540564 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540564 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18637853-4229-5999-8b4d-c1dd1a465baa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540579 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{125\\pi }{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540579 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540579 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e7fe586-ba60-562a-b399-5620cba64770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540589 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [