edb/ref 17592186540786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541044 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541044 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541044 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541044 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ece1f034-27d4-5b04-b263-6e6fc7866f2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541049 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f590a07e-b055-5cd3-bfa4-36805a56228e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541061 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541061 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541061 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-4+5x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0dfc8af8-b45c-5404-a021-aa0515c46816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541061 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+5x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=4.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}x(-4+5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(-4x+5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi ((-2({4}^{2})+\\frac{5}{3}({4}^{3})-\\frac{1}{4}({4}^{4}))-(-2+\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{45}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{45\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541067 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}-3x.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d5c9e76-b23d-5f4b-b9c9-3236b501399a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541067 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(x)=2x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}-3x.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x & ={x}^{2}-3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x & =0 \\\\ x(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=5.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=5.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/d8/d8833c0c6f1becdbe2841f11fae25bb8e558edd06abee6668b2593c4e37c747b.png)\n\n  \n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}-3x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=2x.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}x([2x]-[{x}^{2}-3x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}x(5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}(5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{{5}^{4}}{3}-\\frac{{5}^{4}}{4})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541077 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541077 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/11/11b85e35604a0350a9fd7754dfda481000debb20397d9aa593a1b887409a97c7.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}$ and $y=2x,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4022977e-c101-551c-847a-d892e1fa6f2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541077 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(x)=4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}$ and $g(x)=2x.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =2x \\\\ 8x-{x}^{3}-2x & =0 \\\\ 4x-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x(2-x)(2+x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$ because we are only concerned about $x\\ge 0.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/e0/e03c88c1d24116cc9f68c69150eba39cb6593b4bf6fd4ca9640987ce4ba62299.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=2x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]-[2x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541080 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541080 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541080 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541080 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x$ and $y=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.$ Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541080 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ae3570d-9f53-5028-8c3b-4778adbb6463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541080 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x & =-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+5x & =-2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+5x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/d1/d19b70836b7b377c498fa505868563115706b588b0a2216d09c6ec738f746bbd.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=g(x)=-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([-{x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x]-[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\frac{32}{5}+8) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541089 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/17/179894a17a2aadf5ba9f463ae3faf2e5836f7b268066f8f56de0f3cf3ff2d8fa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6674be9e-fa9a-51c5-9fa3-be41c5ad9ead" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541089 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(2-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541109 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541109 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541109 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541109 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541109 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "845bcab8-f4ff-59be-a2f2-b0d273cf58d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541109 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(4-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=4.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}x(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {({x}^{4}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi [({4}^{4}-\\frac{{4}^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (256-\\frac{1024}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{512\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541117 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541117 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541117 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-6+5x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541117 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0beb016-ee67-53e9-a1ae-c396296738c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541117 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=2$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=2$ to $x=3.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}x(-6+5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}(-6x+5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-3(3)^{2}+\\frac{5(3)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4})-(-3(2)^{2}+\\frac{5(2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4})] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541134 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541134 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/51/51143fcae2b39d210e9274fd26363dfdc5cc72e289aea9f524f34f0e92b82b09.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce012b33-e22a-528a-bd43-8ae57ebb53bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541134 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{5})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541142 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541142 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541142 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541142 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f5/f5b69213924ffd4f7aef6acf1837fd05fbf6b0fd684de12a19f067e452fe42ea.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x$ and $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541142 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc6488b4-82a0-5ac0-9b15-2d4fb9f9b1f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541142 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ x & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4} \\\\ 4x-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x(2-x)(2+x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$ because we are only concerned about $x\\ge 0.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/cc/ccbaa663f6f108322f7bafd4a192a5b9acdf913b61b8319048e92b7885458a91.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([x]-[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
